<title>MEXICO: Mexico expects 0.3 pct GDP budget deficit to 2000.</title>
<headline>Mexico expects 0.3 pct GDP budget deficit to 2000.</headline>
<dateline>MEXICO CITY 1997-06-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico said on Tuesday it projected its budget deficit will narrow annually during the next three calendar years, falling to 0.3 percent of gross domestic product in the year 2000.</p>
<p>Outlining its economic plans in its National Program for Financing Development 1997-2000, the government gave the following breakdown of planned public sector deficits as a percentage of GDP:  </p>
<p>			     1998     1999     2000</p>
<p> BUDGET BALANCE	  -1.3     -0.8     -0.3</p>
<p>    BUDGET REVENUES    22.0     22.2     22.4</p>
<p>     OIL SECTOR	   8.9	8.6	8.4</p>
<p>     NON-OIL SECTOR    13.1     13.5     14.0</p>
<p>	 FED GOV'T	  9.5     10.0     10.5</p>
<p>	   TAXATION	 8.0	8.5	9.1</p>
<p>	   NON-TAXATION   1.5	1.5	1.4</p>
<p>	 ORGANISATIONS &amp;</p>
<p>	 BUSINESSES	 3.6	3.5	3.5</p>
<p>   NET SPENDING	  23.3     22.9     22.7</p>
<p>   PROGRAMMED SPENDING 16.3     16.1     16.0</p>
<p>	 DIFFERENCES ON</p>
<p>	 PAYMENTS	  -0.5     -0.5     -0.5</p>
<p>    PROGRAMMED EARNING 16.8     16.6     16.5</p>
<p>EXCLUDING SOCIAL</p>
<p>    SECURITY	     16.1     15.9     15.8</p>
<p>SOCIAL SECURITY</p>
<p>    REFORM		 0.7	 0.7	0.7</p>
<p>    NON-PROGRAMMED     7.0	 6.8	6.7</p>
<p>    INTEREST	     3.5	 3.2	3.0</p>
<p>    OTHERS		 3.5	 3.6	3.7</p>
<p>((--Robert Kozak, Mexico City newsroom 525 728-9559))</p>
